hi there steve kaufman here  today i want to talk about corrections  correcting people  when they make mistakes in  the process of learning a language  um last few videos i've talked about how  language learning is a  subconscious process how we learn  subconsciously gradually improve without  realizing it that a lot of our  deliberate efforts at learning are  somewhat futile  i did a blog post over at my blog and  i'll leave a link up here  where i quoted some  studies that showed that  uh  what they call form-based instruction or  in other words teaching grammar and  corrections  have relatively little  impact on  the learning process  and i believe this to be the case  and it's funny because that's one of the  comments that seems to  uh elicit the most sort of critical  response from  you the listeners so i wanted to delve a  little further  into this whole issue of corrections and  why i think they are quite unimportant  um  i've been learning romanian  um  and i've been at it for five weeks now  and uh i'm doing quite well i mean it's  i've never learned a language so quickly  partly because it's a romance language  mostly because it's a romance language  uh partly because of how i'm using link  i think i'm using it more and more  efficiently  partly because of the resources that i  found on the internet  having people initially  translate and record a lot of basic  sentences for me which i imported into  link which i listened to i still  listened to  radio romania and the resources there  and and so forth  now um  i'm now speaking to three tutors i talk  three times a week  and i talk  and as i mentioned earlier one of the  tutors  for a while insisted on correcting me  and i finally had to insist with him  that i don't want him to do that because  it basically  saps my enthusiasm for our conversation  i think we're having an interesting  conversation on some subject and he just  wants to correct me  so  we stop doing that we just talk now and  i get my report afterwards with  some of my mistakes which i import and  it  either has some impact or it has  no impact it has some impact it's  content it's it's  part of the whole language environment  but the main reason why i think that  language correction is not a good idea  is because fundamentally i believe  language learning  is something that you have to do  yourself you have to take you the  learner have to take responsibility for  your own  learning  it is true that a lot of people claim  that they like to be corrected  and of course they're trained to like to  be corrected because that's how school  works you write a test you get seven  right and three wrong you get seventy  percent  uh we're all used to that we kind of  think that if we don't have someone  looking over us correcting us  constantly giving us feedback that we  aren't learning but my view to that is  that it's time to grow up and take  responsibility for your own learning  i know as a learner  which things i have trouble with  in romanian they have three versions of  the future  uh they have some structures where you  use the infinitive  and somewhere you use like the  subjunctive to express the future for  example you can say  uh  voy uh avia i will have which is the  infinitive  voy uh voiputia voy uh  where you are now using the subjunctive  and it's so difficult to get that i have  looked at the rules many many times but  the point is that i know it's a weakness  uh as there are many others in romanian  things that are difficult  and so i every so often i refer back to  the grammar  and but when i read and listen i try to  focus on those things  so that i notice them more and then when  i speak i get them wrong  and then i look at the rule again and  then i listen again and i read again and  i pay attention but it's my  responsibility i know that it's a  weakness i don't really need anyone to  correct me because i know more or less  when i get it wrong in a way when i get  it right  i think where correction comes in  is when we are quite advanced in the  language where we have certain  things that we are saying that we don't  realize that we're saying wrong  and so correction  can be useful when the person is already  quite fluent and has certain ingrained  patterns that may come from their native  language which they don't realize that  they're getting wrong all the time but  for a long time in the language we know  very well that we have trouble with  tenses we know that we have trouble with  pronouns we have trouble with cases in  russian we know where our problems are  and so we don't need to be corrected all  the time if we are speaking  then we should be enjoying the process  of speaking  um  and get some rhythm and curse elicit a  response from the native speaker and and  hopefully we pay attention to what the  native speaker says  and pick up on how they say things  correctly because if we're talking about  a particular subject it's very likely  that some of the same vocabulary and  some of the same grammatical structures  will come back to us in what the native  speaker says but we don't i don't think  it's  constructive or productive to correct  the learner and i think particularly the  learner shouldn't be expecting the  correction all the time the learner  should take the initiative to go there  and dig  dig  into the content when they're listening  and reading  dig into whatever grammar resources  they're using whether online or in  little book  be aware of where their problems are and  then go back and dig and that constant  that sort of  virtuous circle  of  noticing things not being clear not  understanding getting it wrong  going back to the grammar resource  looking at it still not quite  understanding or understanding but still  getting wrong and gradually over time we  get better but it's our responsibility  it's not  i don't think that effective and the  research shows  that a major emphasis on grammar  correction and grammar instruction  doesn't work and of course we've seen it  in our schools where that's the major  emphasis so  i think that for an independent learner  that  correction or relying on correction is a  bad habit  and i have not been corrected very much  in my romanian i've been at it for five  weeks  when i look up an article now on uh  radio romania  because they have a number of news  articles that are printed like  literally there is hardly a word that i  don't know there like i can read all  those articles when i listen i  understand a lot of it now i was  recently sent an audio book  and the pdf of a novel  now there there's a lot of words that i  don't know so that's a whole new area  now but because i've been focused on  news and  economics and stuff that like that you  know i'm i'm doing fine there and i  can't use all those words myself but  with time i will be able to and i've  achieved that in five weeks if i had  gone to a traditional program with a  traditional instructor instructor taking  us through the different parts of speech  and the different structures in the  language and correcting us and giving a  test i mean i don't think that in six  months  i would be as far along as i am  now after five weeks  uh and so you may say yes you're  experienced and you're better at  languages than some people and which may  be true but more than anything i'm  experienced at language learning and i  know  that this massive input  constantly getting more of the language  in you  struggling to learn the words  starting now to use it without worrying  too much  about  grammar  certainly this is nothing one of my pet  peeves is the people who ask questions  why is it this why is it that can you  also say it this way can you how about  this other way and then they'll come up  with some totally strange way of using  this language and saying is this correct  i i just don't understand people who do  that  go to the source  go to the  listening and reading that you're doing  let it come in  when you have questions  have by all means have a grammar  resource handy that you go to a grammar  is not a curriculum  grammar is a reference source it's  something you go to from time to time  and often but it's not something that  you learn  one step at a time at least not in my  experience and of course when i speak  about language learning i talk about my  own experience and other people may do  it differently but i at least  want to make it very clear as to why i  feel this way about correction how that  it's not very useful and for learners to  be expecting or relying on correction i  think is an impediment to language  learning there you have it  look forward to your comments bye for  now

